latives of such former  journey to the microcosm stars as hydra  and the starlet cnm name and like their  cydarian relatives corals have simple  bodies through the middle of the animal  is a gastrovascular cavity that opens to  a mouth lined with a ring of tentacles  that sweep through the water in search  of food  helping them in their search for food  are special cells on their tentacles  called nidocites which are loaded with  toxins  the type of corals that we are showing  you today are different from the types  that assemble the vast majority of reefs  those corals are often called hard  corals or stony corals thanks to the  hard exteriors the corals make for  themselves out of calcium carbonate but  the corals that james looked at under  the microscope represent the softer side  of their family like this xenia with its  bluish arms moving with the water around  it  these soft corals still live in reefs  but they don't form the calcium  carbonate structures that their stony  counterparts do  many species of corals stony and soft  alike share another trait the  dinoflagellates though in this symbiotic  context they are known by another name  zooxanthellae  you can see their brown bodies inside  the soft coral xenia in some corals the  presence of these zooxanthellae might  not be immediately obvious like this  zoanthus which is under white light  those brown dots that were so  immediately obvious when we zoomed on  the xenia are not so obvious now  so where are the zuisantelli  to find them we take advantage of the  photosynthetic pigment inside of  zooxanthellae chlorophyll which glows  red when it is excited with red light  when we illuminate the coral with this  red light we see the algae then as a  bright red version of themselves tucked  into different parts of the coral  [Music]  the oldest coral fossils that have been  found are around 400 million years old  however corals haven't had algae living  inside of them the whole time now it's  hard to pin down 